WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.160
Christopher Lee is most familiar to the world
as a consummate actor with a deep, rich voice

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:05.720
and a towering presence.

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:09.650
In ten hammer Studios films he became the
embodiment of evil, inhabiting the character

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:12.590
of Count von Dracula with a sinister charm.

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:17.010
Then, breaking the mould, he went on to play
some of the most iconic characters in cinematic

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:18.010
history.

00:00:18.010 --> 00:00:22.720
Yet, who would have known that the great character
actor was a former WW2 RAF Intelligence Officer

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:27.190
and Nazi hunter who had been witness to unspeakable
atrocities.

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:32.310
In this week’s Biographics we pierce the
celluloid to discover the real Christopher

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:37.929
Lee.

00:00:37.929 --> 00:00:51.289
Early Life
Christopher Frank Carandini Lee was born on

00:00:51.289 --> 00:00:54.910
May 27th, 1922 in Belgravia, London.

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:59.530
His father, Geoffrey, was a hero of the First
World War, having been decorated for gallantry

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:00.640
at the Battle of the Somme.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:04.510
At the time of his son’s birth he was a
Lieutenant Colonel on the King’s Royal Rifle

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:05.510
Corps.

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:09.550
Geoffrey was married to an Italian countess
by the name of Estelle Marie.

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:14.290
Blessed with great beauty, Estelle’s heritage
traced all the way back to Emperor Charlemagne.

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:18.830
As a result, the Lee household was frequently
visited by high ranking figures of European

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:24.050
nobility, and the young Christopher came into
contact with some very interesting and important

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:25.050
people.

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:29.070
Christopher was the Lee’s second child,
with a sister, Xandra, having been born in

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:30.070
1917.

00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:34.470
However, the marriage was not a happy one
and, when the boy was just four-years-old

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:35.770
his parents separated.

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:40.281
For the next two years, the children lived
with Estelle in Wengen, Switzerland, while

00:01:40.281 --> 00:01:42.700
their father remained in the Belgravia house.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.500
After two years the marriage was officially
ended.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:49.860
While in Switzerland, Estelle enrolled her
son in a preschool called Miss Fischer’s

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:52.240
Academy, which was located in Territet.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:56.900
It was here that Christopher was given his
first acting role, appearing as the lead character

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:58.630
in the play Rumpelstiltskin.

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:01.640
In 1928, Estelle and the children returned
to England.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:05.130
Christopher was put into Wagner’s private
school in Queen’s Gate, London.

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:09.489
In 1929, Estelle married a banker by the name
of Harcourt George-St-Croix Rose.

00:02:09.489 --> 00:02:13.760
Lee’s step-father happened to be the uncle
of a young writer by the name of Ian Fleming,

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:19.079
who would go on to create, not only James
Bond, but also Bond’s arch enemy, Scaramanga

00:02:19.079 --> 00:02:25.340
or The Man With the Golden Gun, who Lee would
famously play on film a half century later.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:27.569
Following the marriage, the family moved to
Fulham.

00:02:27.569 --> 00:02:32.180
At the age of nine Christopher was enrolled
at Summer Fields, a boarding school in Oxford.

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:34.300
Lee enjoyed his time at Summer Fields.

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:38.379
He made friends quickly and proved himself
to be a gifted and studious pupil.

00:02:38.379 --> 00:02:42.549
Among his closest friends at Summer fields
was Patrick MacNee, who would also carve a

00:02:42.549 --> 00:02:47.999
career as an actor and find fame as secret
agent John Steed in The Avengers. Lee and

00:02:47.999 --> 00:02:51.819
MacNee both enjoyed acting and appeared together
in a number of school plays.

00:02:51.819 --> 00:02:56.629
Summer Fields was a feeding school to Eton
College, and in 1935, Christopher sat the

00:02:56.629 --> 00:02:59.689
scholarship exam and was interviewed by the
school provost.

00:02:59.689 --> 00:03:04.549
However, his maths ability let him down and
he missed out on a scholarship by one place.

00:03:04.549 --> 00:03:08.080
If he was to attend Eton, his family would
have to pay his way.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:11.999
Despite his mother’s urgings, his step-father
was unwilling to foot the bill, so he ended

00:03:11.999 --> 00:03:13.760
up at Wellington College in Berkshire.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:17.889
It was at Wellington that Lee proved himself
to be a brilliant linguist.

00:03:17.889 --> 00:03:22.010
He mastered French, Spanish, Italian and German
and gained a passing knowledge of Swedish,

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:23.079
Greek and Russian.

00:03:23.079 --> 00:03:27.739
Yet, despite his academic prowess, he chafed
under the harsh discipline that was meted

00:03:27.739 --> 00:03:28.739
out at the school.

00:03:28.739 --> 00:03:33.170
He hated the regular parades and weapons training
that became more and more regular as talk

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:35.870
of war became more frequent in political circles.

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:39.700
So, it was with some relief that he had to
leave Wellington when his step-father went

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:43.359
bankrupt in 1939.

00:03:43.359 --> 00:03:54.059
Making a Living
The seventeen-year-old’s education was over.

00:03:54.059 --> 00:03:56.260
He was now expected to make his own way in
the world.

00:03:56.260 --> 00:04:00.700
But he entered the job market just as the
summer holidays were beginning and there were

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:02.079
no prospects.

00:04:02.079 --> 00:04:05.889
After a month of fruitless job hunting he
was allowed to join his sister who was on

00:04:05.889 --> 00:04:08.019
holiday on the French Riviera.

00:04:08.019 --> 00:04:11.809
While there, Lee heard news that the last
public execution in France was to take place

00:04:11.809 --> 00:04:12.980
in Paris.

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:16.269
Along with thousands of others he took the
trip to witness the gruesome event.

00:04:16.269 --> 00:04:19.400
From Paris, he travelled to Menton in southern
France.

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:23.530
There he stayed with an exiled Russian princely
family with ties to his mother.

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:28.500
He enjoyed his time there and wanted to stay,
but, with Europe about to erupt into warfare,

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:32.060
it was decided that he would be better off
back in the relative safety of London.

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:35.210
For a short time, he worked as a clerk in
a shipping line office.

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:39.300
When war broke out in September, 1939, Lee
was eager to do his part.

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:42.470
Too young to join up in the regular army,
he teamed up with some friends and travelled

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:46.620
to Finland with plans to fight aside the Finns
in their struggle against Russia.

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:51.880
The British teens may have had a lot of fighting
spirit, but none of them knew how to ski,

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:55.750
which rendered them virtually useless in the
snow-covered battle terrain.

00:04:55.750 --> 00:05:00.210
They were politely thanked for their assistance
and then strongly encouraged to return to

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:01.210
England.

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:03.360
Back in London, Lee resumed his work as an
office clerk.

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:06.539
In 1940, aged 18, he joined the Home Guard.

00:05:06.539 --> 00:05:09.120
It was around this time that his father, Geoffrey,
died.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:13.879
Though he had great respect for his father’s
wartime endeavours, Christopher had no desire

00:05:13.879 --> 00:05:16.259
to follow his footsteps into the regular army.

00:05:16.259 --> 00:05:21.159
Knowing that conscription was not far away,
he decided to enter the armed services on

00:05:21.159 --> 00:05:22.159
his terms.

00:05:22.159 --> 00:05:28.060
Consequently, he volunteered to the Royal
Air Force.

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:37.750
The War Years
Following his training at the Initial Training

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:40.000
Wing at Paignton, Lee was stationed to Southern
Rhodesia.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:44.710
On the brink of his first solo flying mission
he complained of blackouts and blurred vision.

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:49.539
He was diagnosed with a failed optic nerve
and told that he would never fly again.

00:05:49.539 --> 00:05:53.800
For a number of months, he was transferred
around the RAF service in Africa, but given

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:55.069
nothing meaningful to do.

00:05:55.069 --> 00:05:59.280
Then, out of frustration, he applied to the
RAF Intelligence Service.

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:03.819
Gaining entry to the service, he was posted
as a warder to the Salisbury Prison in Rhodesia.

00:06:03.819 --> 00:06:07.330
From there he was moved to the Suez Canal
Zone, where he was put to work in intelligence

00:06:07.330 --> 00:06:08.939
gathering activities.

00:06:08.939 --> 00:06:14.080
During the North African Campaign, he was
deeply involved as part of No. 260 Squadron

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:18.650
RAF, nearly losing his life when an airfield
was destroyed by a German bombing raid.

00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:19.889
Throughout 1943 and ’44 he was constantly
struck down by malaria, finally being sent

00:06:19.889 --> 00:06:20.889
to Carthage for treatment.

00:06:20.889 --> 00:06:22.949
In the winter of 1943, Lee was part of an
officer’s swap, and he was seconded to the

00:06:22.949 --> 00:06:23.949
army.

00:06:23.949 --> 00:06:28.629
He spent the next year serving with the Gurkhas
of the 8th Indian Infantry Division.

00:06:28.629 --> 00:06:33.000
He saw action in the Battle of Monte Cassino,
almost losing his life when a plane crashed

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:34.000
on take-off.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:38.740
Towards the end of 1944, Lee was promoted
to flight lieutenant and posted to Air Force

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:39.740
HQ.

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:44.349
When the end of hostilities finally came in
April, 1945, he was given a position in the

00:06:44.349 --> 00:06:47.580
Central Registry of War Criminals of Security
Suspects.

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:50.270
He now became a Nazi hunter.

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:54.379
The work was top secret and we have few specifics
about what he did.

00:06:54.379 --> 00:06:56.539
In a later interview however he did say this
. . .

00:06:56.539 --> 00:07:00.620
“We were given dossiers of what they’d
done and told to find them, interrogate them

00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:04.780
as much as we could and hand them over to
the appropriate authority . . . We saw these

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:05.810
concentration camps.

00:07:05.810 --> 00:07:07.530
Some had been cleaned up.

00:07:07.530 --> 00:07:08.530
Some had not.”

00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:20.969
A New Career Beckons
Lee left the army in 1946.

00:07:20.969 --> 00:07:25.039
After what he had experienced over the previous
few years, he could no longer bring himself

00:07:25.039 --> 00:07:26.449
to working in an office.

00:07:26.449 --> 00:07:31.080
He had the opportunity to teach classics at
University, but didn’t fancy that prospect

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:32.080
either.

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:35.560
Unsure what to make of his post-military life,
the twenty-four-year old found himself dining

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:39.860
at the Italian Embassy in London with his
cousin, the Italian ambassador to England,

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:40.860
Nicolo Carandini.

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:45.389
Out of the blue, Carandini looked at Lee and
said, ‘Have you ever thought of being an

00:07:45.389 --> 00:07:46.629
actor?’

00:07:46.629 --> 00:07:51.229
Taken a little off guard, Lee replied, ‘No,
I don’t think so.’

00:07:51.229 --> 00:07:55.789
Carandini then replied, ‘Perhaps, when you
go away, you might want to think about it.’

00:07:55.789 --> 00:07:56.940
Think about he did.

00:07:56.940 --> 00:08:00.479
He decided that he quite liked the idea and
mentioned it to his mother.

00:08:00.479 --> 00:08:03.580
The countess, however, was vehemently opposed.

00:08:03.580 --> 00:08:08.639
A career in acting was beneath their status
she implored and, besides, at 6 feet five

00:08:08.639 --> 00:08:13.520
he was too tall, he was too foreign looking
and it was hardly a steady line of work.

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:18.539
But the more she railed against the acting
idea, the more her son became determined to

00:08:18.539 --> 00:08:19.830
make a success of it.

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:22.491
Towards the end of 1946, he joined the Rank
Company of Youth.

00:08:22.491 --> 00:08:26.990
For the next few years he busied himself learning
the craft of acting.

00:08:26.990 --> 00:08:31.229
He swept the theatre stage, held prompt cards
for the actors on stage and eventually got

00:08:31.229 --> 00:08:32.899
tiny parts himself.

00:08:32.899 --> 00:08:34.970
Then came small roles in films.

00:08:34.970 --> 00:08:39.029
His height did, indeed work against him, with
casting directors struggling to find a place

00:08:39.029 --> 00:08:40.029
for him.

00:08:40.029 --> 00:08:41.719
However, the roles did come.

00:08:41.719 --> 00:08:45.740
Directors managed to find creative ways to
hide his height, such as having him sit during

00:08:45.740 --> 00:08:46.740
his scenes.

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:51.149
Some of his roles in these early years were
uncredited, while others ended up on the cutting

00:08:51.149 --> 00:08:56.649
room floor, but they all added to the skills
that he would display when the big roles eventually

00:08:56.649 --> 00:09:00.310
came his way.

00:09:00.310 --> 00:09:10.430
Hammer Horror
In 1957, Lee was hired by Hammer Film Productions

00:09:10.430 --> 00:09:12.360
for his biggest part yet.

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:16.560
He was to play the monster created by Baron
von Frankenstein, who was to be played by

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:18.820
his good friend Peter Cushing.

00:09:18.820 --> 00:09:23.690
The movie, called The Curse of Frankenstein,
found an immediate audience among lovers of

00:09:23.690 --> 00:09:26.029
horror, who clamoured for more.

00:09:26.029 --> 00:09:30.310
Hammer decided to satisfy the demand with
a cinematic take on the Bram Stoker classic

00:09:30.310 --> 00:09:32.720
Dracula, with Lee in the lead role.

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:37.529
The movie, released in 1958, was a huge hit,
with Lee’s sinister yet charming take on

00:09:37.529 --> 00:09:41.630
the fanged count forever embedding itself
in the public consciousness.

00:09:41.630 --> 00:09:46.990
Dracula was the movie that turned the struggling
36-year-old journeyman actor into a star.

00:09:46.990 --> 00:09:51.600
He later recalled, ‘It brought me a name,
a fan club and a second-hand car, for all

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:53.540
of which I was grateful.’

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:56.989
The Dracula role was both a blessing and a
curse for Lee.

00:09:56.989 --> 00:10:01.390
It inevitably type cast him – and it tied
him to appear in a succession of sequels,

00:10:01.390 --> 00:10:03.640
each one worse than the last.

00:10:03.640 --> 00:10:08.089
When he tried to bring the series to an end
he was, in his own words, ‘blackmailed’

00:10:08.089 --> 00:10:12.589
by Hammer executives, who would remind him
how many people he would put out on the street

00:10:12.589 --> 00:10:14.769
if the next movie was not made.

00:10:14.769 --> 00:10:18.930
Lee played the part of Dracula ten times,
and increasingly grew tired of the shadow

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:20.589
that it cast over his career.

00:10:20.589 --> 00:10:24.540
It took eight years for the second Dracula
to be released, by which time the first movie

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:27.190
had made a tremendous impact all over the
world.

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:31.820
Yet, when Lee was handed the script for the
sequel, he was horrified, not by the murderous

00:10:31.820 --> 00:10:36.060
things that his character was asked to do,
but by the terrible dialogue that he had been

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:37.060
given.

00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:42.980
As a result, he refused to do the lines, which
is why Dracula does not say a word in 1965’s

00:10:42.980 --> 00:10:47.089
Dracula: Prince of Darkness. Lee was also
concerned that the character of Dracula was

00:10:47.089 --> 00:10:51.959
getting further and further away from what
Bram Stoker had in mind when he wrote the

00:10:51.959 --> 00:10:54.810
book.

00:10:54.810 --> 00:11:04.970
Serious Acting
According to the man himself, Lee’s real

00:11:04.970 --> 00:11:09.579
career as a serious actor started when he
was cast as the Marquis de Sade in A Tale

00:11:09.579 --> 00:11:11.480
of Two Cities in 1958.

00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:14.130
This was, in fact, his 43rd movie.

00:11:14.130 --> 00:11:19.200
Other serious roles followed, but he was unable
to escape the horror genre for over a decade,

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:23.840
appearing in a slew of largely forgettable
blood and guts flicks, both for Hammer Studios

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:25.810
and other production companies.

00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:30.970
In 1962, Lee auditioned for a role in the
World War Two classic The Longest Day.

00:11:30.970 --> 00:11:35.160
It is ironic that, despite his distinguished
service for his country during the actual

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:38.981
war, he was turned down because it was felt
that audiences wouldn’t accept him as a

00:11:38.981 --> 00:11:40.209
military type.

00:11:40.209 --> 00:11:45.990
From 1965 until 1969, Lee, despite his obvious
English bearing, starred in a series of movies

00:11:45.990 --> 00:11:49.700
in which he played the villainous oriental
Fu Manchu.

00:11:49.700 --> 00:11:53.899
His skill as an actor, along with long hours
in the makeup chair, allowed him to breathe

00:11:53.899 --> 00:11:56.330
life into the role of the criminal mastermind.

00:11:56.330 --> 00:12:00.330
In 1970, Lee picked up a single day’s work
as the narrator in a film about the Marquis

00:12:00.330 --> 00:12:02.440
De Sade entitled Eugenie.

00:12:02.440 --> 00:12:06.410
It was only when the movie was released that
he realized that he was appearing in a soft

00:12:06.410 --> 00:12:09.990
porn film, loaded with sex scenes that were
shot when he wasn’t around.

00:12:09.990 --> 00:12:13.940
He was embarrassed and angry, but he could
do nothing about it.

00:12:13.940 --> 00:12:18.480
Lee credits his role as Sherlock Holmes’
smarter older brother, Mycroft, in 1970’s

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:22.320
The Private Life of Sherlock Holmes as the
role that allowed him to finally break free

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:25.449
from being typecast as the ‘horror’ guy.

00:12:25.449 --> 00:12:29.470
Three years later he played Lord Summerisle
in what he considered the best movie he ever

00:12:29.470 --> 00:12:30.910
made, The Wicker Man.

00:12:30.910 --> 00:12:35.160
Lee was so taken with the book Ritual upon
which the movie is based that he offered his

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:37.089
services free of charge.

00:12:37.089 --> 00:12:40.959
The film has become a cult classic and is
considered one of the best British movies

00:12:40.959 --> 00:12:41.970
ever made.

00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:45.779
In the mid-70’s, Lee appeared as Comte de
Rochefort in The Three Musketeers, and its

00:12:45.779 --> 00:12:47.630
sequel The Four Musketeers.

00:12:47.630 --> 00:12:51.010
During a fight scene he received a knee injury,
which would plague him for the rest of his

00:12:51.010 --> 00:12:52.010
life.

00:12:52.010 --> 00:12:55.899
Back in the early ‘60’s, Lee’s step-cousin
Ian Fleming, who had made it big as an author,

00:12:55.899 --> 00:13:00.010
had offered him the role of the villainous
title character in the movie adaptation of

00:13:00.010 --> 00:13:02.290
his James Bond novel, Dr. No.

00:13:02.290 --> 00:13:03.620
Lee accepted on the spot.

00:13:03.620 --> 00:13:07.420
However, Fleming hadn’t cleared Lee with
the producers and they had already chosen

00:13:07.420 --> 00:13:08.420
their man.

00:13:08.420 --> 00:13:13.029
A dozen years later, Lee finally got his chance
when he landed the role of Francisco Scaramanga

00:13:13.029 --> 00:13:16.089
in the tenth Bond flick, The Man With The
Golden Gun.

00:13:16.089 --> 00:13:20.769
Lee breathed life into the character, who
Fleming had written as a rather bland bad

00:13:20.769 --> 00:13:22.770
guy lacking real depth.

00:13:22.770 --> 00:13:27.340
The writer was delighted with the portrayal,
claiming that Lee had created the sinister

00:13:27.340 --> 00:13:30.610
counterpart to James Bond.

00:13:30.610 --> 00:13:42.399
Coming to America
By 1977, Lee had established a reputation

00:13:42.399 --> 00:13:46.279
as a fine actor who could take on any role
and make it his own.

00:13:46.279 --> 00:13:50.420
Still, he was concerned that, if he remained
in England, he would eventually be lured back

00:13:50.420 --> 00:13:52.440
into the dark tunnel of horror movies.

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:58.779
Avoiding that fate was the prime motivation
for his move to the United States in 1977.

00:13:58.779 --> 00:14:03.110
His first American role was a departure from
anything that had gone before – the modern-day

00:14:03.110 --> 00:14:05.040
disaster Airport ’77.

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:09.050
The following year he went even further beyond
his comfort zone by appearing as the guest

00:14:09.050 --> 00:14:12.579
host on the NBC live comedy Saturday Night
Live.

00:14:12.579 --> 00:14:16.649
He remembered the experience as the most terrifying
hour and a half of his life.

00:14:16.649 --> 00:14:21.709
That night he impressed many people, including
budding director Steven Spielberg, who promptly

00:14:21.709 --> 00:14:25.100
signed him up for his next project, the period
comedy 1941.

00:14:25.100 --> 00:14:30.730
Lee had the chance to follow up with another
comedy, a spoof of Airport ’77 called Airplane!

00:14:30.730 --> 00:14:33.759
But he turned the role down and it went to
Leslie Nielsen, who made it his own.

00:14:33.759 --> 00:14:36.850
Lee would refer to this decision as ‘a big
mistake.’

00:14:36.850 --> 00:14:41.550
Through the 80’s and ‘90’s, Lee appeared
in a broad spectrum of movie genres from dramas

00:14:41.550 --> 00:14:43.560
to comedies and even musicals.

00:14:43.560 --> 00:14:48.810
He also made regular appearances on the small
screen, for both British and American TV audiences.

00:14:48.810 --> 00:14:54.130
In 1998, Lee was controversially cast as Muhammad
Ali Jinnah, the founder of Pakistan in the

00:14:54.130 --> 00:14:55.130
movie Jinnah.

00:14:55.130 --> 00:15:00.500
It was to be his most demanding role, and
the one that carried the most responsibility.

00:15:00.500 --> 00:15:05.920
For ten weeks, he acted the part of the father
of the Pakistani nation on location, as thousands

00:15:05.920 --> 00:15:08.620
of locals scrutinized his every move.

00:15:08.620 --> 00:15:13.750
The vast majority of them were won over by
the consummate actor, amazed and delighted

00:15:13.750 --> 00:15:15.560
with his ability to ‘become’ Jinnah.

00:15:15.560 --> 00:15:22.620
The movie was hugely important to Lee but
it did not achieve widespread commercial success.

00:15:22.620 --> 00:15:34.500
A Lifelong Dream Fulfilled
Lee had been a lifelong fan of the J.R.R.

00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:37.920
Tolkien Lord of the Rings series of books,
reading them annually.

00:15:37.920 --> 00:15:42.210
He had once actually met Tolkien and for decades
he had harbored the desire to play the role

00:15:42.210 --> 00:15:43.360
of Gandalf.

00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:48.319
What he was offered, and immediately accepted,
was the part of Saruman in a trilogy to be

00:15:48.319 --> 00:15:51.459
directed by New Zealand director Peter Jackson.

00:15:51.459 --> 00:15:55.890
The role brought him to a new generation of
fans, who were enthralled with his commanding

00:15:55.890 --> 00:15:56.890
performance.

00:15:56.890 --> 00:16:00.319
Those who were privileged to work with him
on the set were also captivated by the Christopher

00:16:00.319 --> 00:16:01.319
Lee aura.

00:16:01.319 --> 00:16:04.660
In one scene, Saruman was to be stabbed in
the back and director Jackson coached Lee

00:16:04.660 --> 00:16:06.920
to scream as the blade entered his body.

00:16:06.920 --> 00:16:10.920
Lee turned to Jackson and asked him if he’d
ever actually seen a man get stabbed in the

00:16:10.920 --> 00:16:11.920
back.

00:16:11.920 --> 00:16:15.990
Jackson admitted that he hadn’t, to which
Lee said, ‘Well, I have, and I know what

00:16:15.990 --> 00:16:16.990
to do.’

00:16:16.990 --> 00:16:20.139
He went on to explain that such a wound would
puncture the lungs, preventing the person

00:16:20.139 --> 00:16:21.250
from screaming.

00:16:21.250 --> 00:16:24.370
At best they would let out a quiet groan.

00:16:24.370 --> 00:16:26.620
With this revelation, the set went quiet.

00:16:26.620 --> 00:16:30.800
All present knew that they were in the presence
of a man who had lived through things that

00:16:30.800 --> 00:16:32.139
they could only imagine.

00:16:32.139 --> 00:16:36.410
The success of the Lord of Rings movies led
to Lee’s insertion into the Star Wars franchise,

00:16:36.410 --> 00:16:38.690
where he played the evil Count Dooku.

00:16:38.690 --> 00:16:42.440
Throughout the 2000’s he also appeared in
a string of Tim Burton movies, including Sleepy

00:16:42.440 --> 00:16:48.200
Hollow and Sweeney Todd: The Demon Barber
of Fleet Street.

00:16:48.200 --> 00:16:59.000
The Waning Years
In 2011, Lee embraced his roots by appearing

00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:03.810
in his first Hammer film in 35 years, The
Resident, alongside Hilary Swank.

00:17:03.810 --> 00:17:07.770
While filming in Mexico, he suffered a back
injury and had to undergo surgery.

00:17:07.770 --> 00:17:11.459
As a result, he was unable to play a leading
role in the long awaited sequel to The Wicker

00:17:11.459 --> 00:17:13.470
Man, entitled The Wicker Tree.

00:17:13.470 --> 00:17:17.880
However, he was determined to appear in the
film and was given a small role as the mentor

00:17:17.880 --> 00:17:19.939
of the character he was meant to play.

00:17:19.939 --> 00:17:24.789
Although in his late 80’s, Lee was insistent
that he had no intentions of retiring from

00:17:24.789 --> 00:17:25.789
acting.

00:17:25.789 --> 00:17:29.690
He often said that making movies had never
been a job to him – it was his life, it

00:17:29.690 --> 00:17:32.390
gave him meaning and purpose.

00:17:32.390 --> 00:17:37.230
With respiratory problems and a dodgy heart
he couldn’t accept as many roles as in earlier

00:17:37.230 --> 00:17:38.230
times.

00:17:38.230 --> 00:17:42.880
Still he kept busy with voice-over and narration
work and at least one major production each

00:17:42.880 --> 00:17:43.880
year.

00:17:43.880 --> 00:17:47.700
His final movie was an independent production
called Angels of Notting Hill.

00:17:47.700 --> 00:17:52.110
Christopher Lee died on June 27th, 2015, at
the age of 93.

00:17:52.110 --> 00:17:54.360
The official cause of death was heart failure.

00:17:54.360 --> 00:17:59.940
In the tributes that flowed he was recognized
as appearing in 208 films, more than anyone

00:17:59.940 --> 00:18:01.260
else in history.

00:18:01.260 --> 00:18:07.059
Those myriad roles allow him to live on, enabling
his millions of fans, both old and new to

00:18:07.059 --> 00:18:35.600
experience the presence of a true giant of

00:18:35.600 --> 00:18:43.680
the cinema.

